  <h2>How Macros Convert from Calories</h2>
  <p>The arithmetic is fixed by biochemistry. Macronutrients provide energy at these rates:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Protein:</strong> 4 kcal per gram</li>
    <li><strong>Carbohydrates:</strong> 4 kcal per gram</li>
    <li><strong>Fat:</strong> 9 kcal per gram</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Given a calorie target and a percentage split, the gram targets are:</p>
  <p><code>protein_g = (calories × protein%) / 4</code></p>
  <p><code>carbs_g = (calories × carbs%) / 4</code></p>
  <p><code>fat_g = (calories × fat%) / 9</code></p>

  <h2>Common Macro Strategies</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Protein</th><th>Carbs</th><th>Fat</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Balanced</td><td>30%</td><td>40%</td><td>30%</td><td>General health</td></tr>
      <tr><td>High-protein</td><td>35%</td><td>35%</td><td>30%</td><td>Weight loss, muscle preservation</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Low-carb</td><td>30%</td><td>20%</td><td>50%</td><td>Glycemic control, weight loss</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Mediterranean</td><td>20%</td><td>50%</td><td>30%</td><td>Long-term metabolic health</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Keto</td><td>25%</td><td>5%</td><td>70%</td><td>Epilepsy, refractory metabolic conditions</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>When High Protein Matters Most</h2>
  <p>Protein needs scale up during caloric restriction. When you eat less, your body draws on both fat and muscle for energy. Adequate protein intake (1.2–1.6 g per kg of target body weight per day, up to 1.6–2.2 g/kg during active weight loss) signals the body to preserve muscle mass even as fat mass declines.</p>
  <p>High-protein splits are the standard recommendation during weight loss for this reason. The 35/35/30 split (or even 40/30/30 for very lean targets) gives 105 g protein at 1,200 kcal or 175 g protein at 2,000 kcal — within the protective range for most adults.</p>

  <h2>Low-Carb and Keto</h2>
  <p>Very-low-carbohydrate diets (&lt;50 g carbs/day) induce nutritional ketosis — the metabolic state in which the body shifts from glucose to ketone bodies for fuel. Therapeutic ketogenic diets are well-established for refractory epilepsy and are increasingly studied for type 2 diabetes and certain neurological conditions.</p>
  <p>For general weight loss, low-carb diets produce similar results to low-fat diets on average (per multiple meta-analyses), but individual responses vary substantially. Some people thrive on low-carb; others find the restriction unsustainable. Macro strategy should match individual preference and metabolic context.</p>

  <h2>Mediterranean-Style Eating</h2>
  <p>The Mediterranean dietary pattern — rich in olive oil, vegetables, legumes, fish, and whole grains — is the most consistently supported eating pattern in long-term observational research for cardiovascular and metabolic outcomes. The macro profile (20P / 50C / 30F) reflects this: more carbohydrates than low-carb diets, but with emphasis on fiber-rich sources and unsaturated fats.</p>

  <h2>Should You Track Macros at All?</h2>
  <p>Macro tracking is most useful during a structured weight-loss phase or recomposition. Outside those windows, habit-based eating patterns (whole foods, adequate protein, mostly plants) generally outperform obsessive tracking for long-term adherence and psychological well-being. Use the calculator as a planning tool, not a daily ledger.</p>

